WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.420
- Our next presenter is Chitra Ramalingam,

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.190
who is lecturer in History
of Science and Medicine,

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:13.440
as well as History.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.470
Professor Ramalingam is
a historian of science

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:17.480
and a historian of photography.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.450
Her research, teaching and
curatorial work range broadly

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:23.990
across topics in science and
visual and material culture,

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.580
with a particular focus on
the visual culture of physics

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.760
in the 19th and 20th centuries.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.030
On 19th century ways of seeing,

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:34.170
especially optical
illusions, optical toys,

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.230
and the prehistory of cinema.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:40.210
The early history of photography.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:43.220
Changing discourses about
photography as art or science

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.780
and Decolonial Museum Practice.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:46.667
Her first book,

00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:49.230
"To see a spark: Experiment
and visual experience

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:51.480
in Victorian science," uses the history

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.040
of a single scientific
object, the electric spark,

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.460
to track how physicists
manage their visual experience

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.120
in the laboratory.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.700
She has co-edited a volume
of essays by art historians

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.430
and historians of science
on the photographic pioneer

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.130
and Victorian gentlemen of science,

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:07.743
William Henry Fox Talbot.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.260
Titled, "William Henry Fox
Talbot: Beyond Photography."

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.540
And co-curated an
exhibition on Talbot in 2010

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.540
at the Wren Library
University of Cambridge.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.720
She's curated exhibitions
on early photography

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.030
on antiquarianism and
architectural photography

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:28.030
and on social portraiture in
post 1945 British photography.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:31.010
Ongoing projects include a
book project on the laboratory

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:34.130
as image archive in the
Modern Physical Sciences.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:37.087
A material history of Talbot's
photographic publication,

00:01:37.087 --> 00:01:39.160
"The Pencil of Nature," in collaboration

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.420
with conservation scientists
at Yale's institution

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.567
for the preservation of cultural heritage.

00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:44.877
"Fixing and Fading."

00:01:44.877 --> 00:01:47.560
Photographic histories and exhibition

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.790
and book on the science
and aesthetics of fading

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:52.260
in 19th Century and
contemporary photography.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.740
And "Out of Place," a series
of experimental installations

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.330
involving re-situating objects

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.250
from Yale's natural history collections

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.280
about which we'll hear more.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.486
Welcome Professor Ramalingam.

00:02:03.486 --> 00:02:07.060
(audience clapping)

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:07.893
Thank you.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:14.203
- Hi, everybody.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.220
Hold on. Let me just get situated here.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.560
Thank you so much to the
organizers of this event

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.270
for giving us all the opportunity

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.460
to think broadly and
humanistically together

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.210
across Yale collections and resources,

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:33.063
and I'm especially glad to
be following Ned Cooke who's,

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:37.051
I've had some of the most kind of humbling

00:02:37.051 --> 00:02:39.420
and illuminating experiences
that I've had at Yale

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.890
have been in the teachings
that he described

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:42.990
to organizing earlier.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:48.450
So, I wanna talk to you
all today about some work

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:52.130
that emerged out of my own
interdisciplinary practice.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:54.190
As a curator and an academic,

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:55.530
working between History of Science,

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.150
History of Art and Museum Studies.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:02:59.510
A few years ago,

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:02.900
I started a project which
I called "Out of Place,"

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.900
which is a kind of ongoing
research engagement

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:06.613
with Yale's varied collections.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:09.580
And in this project, I'm working with

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:11.640
a number of various
wonderful collaborators,

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.720
both on and off campus

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.810
to bring seemingly
unconnected, unrelated objects

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:20.090
from the universities, museums
of science, art and culture

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.430
into conversation with one another.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:24.780
And this project will result in a series

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.710
of online publications and displays

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:28.890
over the next two or three years.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.670
There's one plan for the
Yale University Art Gallery.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.340
Hopefully, the Peabody Museum
when they re-open in 2024

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.560
and also at the Tarq Gallery in Mumbai

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.720
and hopefully other venues as well.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:41.650
So, today I wanna explain
to you all the process

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:45.110
and the practice behind the
first outcome of this project,

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:47.700
which I've called, "A British Mineralogy,"

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.150
in which I collaborated
with an Indian artist

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:52.930
based in Delhi named Garima Gupta.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:56.120
This project explores the
histories and layered geographies

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.280
and visual landscapes of minerals

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:00.763
at Yale's Peabody Museum
of Natural History.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:06.070
So, in a natural history display

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:08.910
like this classic teaching
display of minerals

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.400
that you can see on the left
from Yale's Peabody Museum,

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.910
natural objects like minerals
from across the globe

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:19.400
are typically identified with
a specific locality of origin.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.590
So, the one place which it is known

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:23.660
to have occurred in nature.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:25.970
All other places through which that object

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.460
may have passed on route to the museum,

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:31.070
on its passage through
human culture and history

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:32.383
are not usually relevant.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:35.790
In the collections of the
Yale Center for British Art,

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:37.160
which you can see on the right,

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:38.880
and where I worked as photography curator

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.770
until a few months ago,

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:43.710
there are objects made in
places around the British Isles,

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.880
but also all around the world

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:47.530
made by people of many backgrounds

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:50.963
become notionally British
by virtue of being there.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.080
Geopolitical relations for
certain periods of time,

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.500
bound those people in places to Britain,

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:01.570
to its state, its fortunes
and its aesthetic styles,

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:04.530
sometimes creating rich hybrid
styles like the so-called

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:06.920
Company School of Indian Painting Masters

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:09.057
such as Shaikh Zain ud-Din.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:13.130
Natural history museums
of course are understood

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:16.140
to tell us powerful stories about nature

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:19.120
while we go to art museums to
hear and see powerful stories

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.610
about culture and identity.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:23.850
Now, this project arose from a number

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:28.440
of generative dissonances,
I guess I would say,

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.370
that I experienced during
my four years as a curator

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:32.360
at the Yale Center for British Art.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.140
So on the one hand,
disciplinary dissonance

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:37.180
as a cultural historian of science

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:39.140
employed in an art museum.

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:40.580
But then on a more personal level,

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:44.210
also as a diasporic
Indian-American at an institution

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:46.500
whose unstable vision of British art

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.160
depended so much on
exoticized images of people

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:51.890
in places of the global South.

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:53.850
So, as I kind of was
conducting my work there

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:54.770
on the photography collection,

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:56.980
which is not related to
what I'm showing you today,

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:59.210
I kept thinking about what other histories

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:01.370
were entangled with the
history of British art

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:03.060
within the walls of that institution,

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:05.830
but were unable or unavailable rather

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.413
via the museum's fine art collections,

00:06:08.413 --> 00:06:12.263
its disciplinary methods and
its conventions of display.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:15.720
Gradually, I came to hone in

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.610
on the Peabody's mineralogy collection

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.890
and it'll become clear why
I turned there in a moment.

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:22.930
I selected a group of 10 mineral specimens

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:25.270
in conversation with the
collection manager at Peabody.

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:28.410
The collection manager for
minerals and meteoritics.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:29.800
These 10 objects are,

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:31.460
it's actually technically
I think 13 objects,

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:32.293
but as you can see,

00:06:32.293 --> 00:06:36.660
some of them are grouped by mineral type.

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:38.520
They were originally
gathered from locations

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:41.530
in the British Isles, but
also in Southern Africa,

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:45.130
Eastern Australia, Sri
Lanka and Western India.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:46.410
They were collected at the Peabody

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.390
from around the mid 19th Century

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:50.760
through the early 21st centuries.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:53.150
Each of them passing first
through British networks

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:56.203
of power, knowledge, value and exchange.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:06:59.560
At its height, as you probably know,

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:01.320
at the start of the 20th century,

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:05.060
the British Empire famously
encompassed the entire earth,

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:06.800
not only with respect to territories

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:09.070
under direct colonial control,

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:12.000
but also wider business
and trade interests.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.300
This is a map of the British-controlled

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.770
global telegraphy network,
around I think 1901.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:19.390
Yeah.

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:21.920
But British Imperial
ambitions did not simply aim

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:23.810
to cover the earth's surface.

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:27.583
They also sought to reach deep
below the Earth's surface.

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:31.630
Mining and engineering in
Britain and its former empire

00:07:31.630 --> 00:07:33.230
are associated with global histories

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:36.540
of migration, inequality,
and exploitation.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:38.340
With the transformation of terrain

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:42.003
and with ecological disasters,
past present and future.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:48.270
So these 10 objects, which
I called migrant minerals,

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:50.530
they contained valuable materials

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:52.880
from the prosaic to the precious.

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:57.200
Graphite tin, copper, diamond and more.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:59.200
And they were brought forth from the earth

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:00.580
through the skilled labor of people

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:03.480
whose lives became entangled with them

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:05.020
and with the technological landscapes

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:06.287
of extraction around them.

00:08:06.287 --> 00:08:10.100
And these are images of the
Kimberly Mines in South Africa.

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:13.083
One of the most notorious
diamond mines in the region.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:16.720
Some of these images are
from Yale collections

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:19.483
like this stereo pair
and the others are not.

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:22.480
Once unearthed,

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:25.550
these objects embarked on
voyages across the globe,

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:28.270
participating in the global
flows of people and commodities

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:32.020
that underlay British power
in a period of high empire.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:33.000
And finally,

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.960
they were assimilated into
a natural history collection

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:37.690
where they shed some of these pasts

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:39.810
in order to become emblems of nature,

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:43.250
apparently outside culture
and outside history.

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:45.343
They had become mineralogical specimens.

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:50.610
So in this project, I was
asking what kind of mineralogy

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:53.870
might embrace and acknowledge
these stories of excavation,

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:56.970
translocation, transformation and arrival?

00:08:56.970 --> 00:09:00.133
Rather than effacing them
in the name of nature.

00:09:01.805 --> 00:09:04.530
And I ended up calling this
project, "A British Mineralogy."

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:06.190
And in this I was inspired

00:09:06.190 --> 00:09:08.600
or more precisely, I was provoked

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:12.960
by the London artist and
naturalist, James Sowerby.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:14.090
In the early 19th Century,

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:16.510
Sowerby published a multi-volume treatise,

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:18.520
which he called, "British Mineralogy."

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:21.290
So, no article. Just British mineralogy,

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:23.610
This was the first major illustrated

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:25.633
mineralogical publication in Europe.

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:29.450
Issued in installments
between 1804 and 1817,

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:34.710
Sowerby's work was visually groundbreaking

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:37.700
with 550 hand-colored engravings

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:40.300
based on individual mineral specimens.

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:41.400
There were two sets of books.

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:45.000
So, the first volumes,
"British Mineralogy,"

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:47.340
were followed a few years
later by their counterpart,

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:51.077
which was a large single volume
called "Exotic Mineralogy."

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:54.450
But Sowerby regularly undermined

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:57.117
his own proposed distinction
between the British

00:09:57.117 --> 00:09:58.760
in his first set of volumes

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:00.893
and the exotic in the final set.

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:05.860
In his text, the words
British, English, Native,

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:08.940
foreign and exotic
carried a lot of weight.

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:10.640
And there were suggestive slippages

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:12.163
and ambiguities throughout.

00:10:13.441 --> 00:10:15.780
Both the British and the
exotic mineralogy project

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:18.840
for Sowerby were limited
to reproducing specimens

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:21.580
that were held in London-based
mineral collections,

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:22.990
which themselves depended of course,

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:26.420
upon networks of British
travelers to distant lands.

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:29.530
And in his project, even a meteorite,

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:31.610
so a piece of stone that was theorized

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:33.010
to a fallen from the heavens,

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:35.630
an object that's
literally from outer space

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:37.210
was found to be British.

00:10:37.210 --> 00:10:39.510
It was included as part
of British mineralogy.

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:41.210
In the fifth volume,

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:44.020
Sowerby observed what has
been thought remarkable

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:46.440
is that this work on British
mineralogy should include

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:49.463
so many subjects previously
considered as foreign.

00:10:51.070 --> 00:10:53.510
So, I was looking through
Sowerby's drawings and his text

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:55.770
while I researched these
10 mineral specimens.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:57.400
And I began to wonder
what it would look like

00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:00.163
to propose a different
kind of British mineralogy.

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:03.610
Sowerby's work was an exercise
in drawing the boundaries

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:06.750
of locale and belonging and Britishness

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:09.470
in a quite different way than
either the Peabody Museum

00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:11.320
or the Yale Center for British Art did.

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:14.320
Or for that matter, quite
different from how a present day

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:16.993
UK immigration officer
would draw those boundaries.

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:20.160
His drawings did not depict the landscapes

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.180
from which those specimens were extracted

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:23.730
or the people involved in doing so,

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:25.710
although his text did
occasionally describe

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:28.090
both the people or the landscapes.

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:29.770
And so, it was at this
point that I realized

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:31.610
that my project could not simply be

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:33.830
what it had been originally
conceived as which was,

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:35.510
it was going to be an
exhibition of minerals

00:11:35.510 --> 00:11:37.380
accompanied by a text that was grounded

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:40.060
in my historical research into the object.

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:42.360
But the more time I spent with Sowerby,

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:45.160
the more urgently I felt
that this project required

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:47.260
a visual practice as a part of it.

00:11:47.260 --> 00:11:50.190
A non-textual way of
restoring a multilayered

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:53.133
sense of place and
belonging to each object.

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:55.490
And at this point in my research,

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:58.860
I had begun to assemble
a kind of visual archive

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:01.860
for each mineral as I researched it.

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:04.460
So, I was finding historical
photographs, diagrams,

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:07.233
maps, printed texts, and other documents,

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:09.510
many of which were in Yale collection,

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:11.380
some of which were things

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:13.130
I had accessed digitally elsewhere.

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:16.180
And all the historical images
that I'm showing you today

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:18.640
are from this archive that I assembled.

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:21.030
And I felt like something
needed to be done with them

00:12:21.030 --> 00:12:21.970
as part of this project.

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:24.870
Something thoughtful that
didn't just reproduce

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:27.730
the visual conventions
of the colonial gaze.

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:30.680
And in any case, I was
sometimes in this research

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:33.360
coming across disturbing
and graphic imagery

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:34.780
of abuse and violence,

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:38.010
including things that I found
in Yale collections again.

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:40.060
Images that I couldn't
bring myself to even save

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:43.700
in my database, let alone
contemplate publishing.

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:45.670
And this was when I came across the work

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:47.480
of the Indian artist and researcher,

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:49.430
Garima Gupta based in Delhi.

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:50.850
And I'm showing you the work here by her

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:53.550
that caused me to reach out
to her in the first place.

00:12:55.705 --> 00:12:58.040
Garima's work is about
the environmental history

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:00.390
of South and Southeast Asia.

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:01.260
She makes drawings,

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:03.550
observational drawings of natural objects.

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:06.430
Rocks, plants, animals.

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:08.260
And then she layers them with drawings

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:11.220
of archival materials that
she's found in her own research.

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:12.760
And she does this in order to make sense

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:14.900
of the visual and ecological legacies

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:16.700
of trade and migration histories

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:19.880
and European colonial
expansion in the region.

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:21.830
And so in the work shown here,

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:23.820
she uses a leaf of paper
from the account books

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:26.850
of an oil palm plantation
company in Malaysia

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:29.060
as the basis for a drawing

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:32.610
of two oil palm flower
heads, male and female.

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:35.640
And she creates these
layered installations

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:37.443
of drawings in light boxes.

00:13:38.770 --> 00:13:41.240
And so you can kind of see
the kind of documentary layer

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:43.310
and the observational drawing layer

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:46.540
kind of merging together
in these light objects.

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:48.870
And she does this in
order to explore visually

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:51.040
a conflict over land use
and ecological change

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:52.490
in the Malaysian archipelago.

00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:55.810
So, I encountered this work
by her and I approached her

00:13:55.810 --> 00:13:57.205
about a year and a half ago.

00:13:57.205 --> 00:13:59.960
This was just before the
pandemic went global.

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:02.310
And she and I began a
conversation about this project

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:05.670
that eventually led to a full
collaboration between us.

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:09.420
And so, the 10 specimens
that you saw earlier,

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:13.530
eventually became over the
course of 2020, 10 drawings.

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:15.120
And I'm just gonna briefly scroll through

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:16.640
all 10 of them right now,

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:18.490
and then I'll dive deeply into one of them

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:20.323
before I finish my talk.

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:24.920
So, I'm gonna finish with the kind of the,

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:27.600
the rest of my talk will
be telling you the story

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:29.550
of this object from the
Peabody Museum here.

00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:32.721
One of the 10 specimens that
I honed in on for this project

00:14:32.721 --> 00:14:35.000
and the first one that I researched.

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:37.240
Now, the only documentation
in the Peabody archives

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:39.320
for this beautiful chunk
of rock and crystal

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:41.330
is the handwritten label
that you can see on it

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:43.400
and that's zoomed in on the right.

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:45.210
This label identifies this mineral

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:47.960
as apophyllite with stilbite.

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:50.440
And the other information
on this label helps us track

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:53.880
the object's origins to
a place called Bor Ghat.

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:56.120
A treacherous pass in the Northern part

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:57.503
of the Western Ghats.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:00.220
The Western Ghats are a mountain range

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:03.620
that run up much of the
Western coast of India.

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:05.280
These basalt mountains were formed

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:07.210
through violent volcanic eruptions

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:09.373
around 66 million years ago.

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:13.430
In the mid 19th Century, they
presented a natural barrier

00:15:13.430 --> 00:15:15.980
between the British-controlled
port city of Bombay

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:19.310
and the rich resources
of the Indian interior.

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:21.130
So, it was nearly impossible for any kind

00:15:21.130 --> 00:15:23.650
of wheeled vehicle to
traverse these mountains.

00:15:23.650 --> 00:15:26.070
And so, if the British
wanted Indian commodities

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:29.780
like silk, opium, cotton spices,

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:30.850
if they wanted these things

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:33.350
to flow efficiently overseas for trade,

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:36.630
they needed a rail line to
cut across the mountains.

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:39.120
And so in 1856, the British established

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:41.510
the Great India Peninsula Railway Company

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:43.293
and they set out to make it happen.

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:46.820
Laying these new rail
lines was the largest

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:49.270
and most labor intensive
civil engineering project

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:51.410
of the British government in India.

00:15:51.410 --> 00:15:53.360
And it coincided with violent rebellions

00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:55.270
that saw the British almost lose control

00:15:55.270 --> 00:15:58.290
of Northern India in 1857.

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:00.620
The project was carried
out over seven years.

00:16:00.620 --> 00:16:02.710
1856 to 1863.

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:04.890
And this place is set
right around the same time

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:07.163
as the construction of the Suez Canal.

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:10.760
The Bor Ghat works actually
employed more workers

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:12.170
than the Suez project.

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:14.623
And ultimately, it
killed many more people.

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:18.940
Building this rail line was dangerous work

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:20.790
in precipitous terrain.

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:23.660
There were severe extremes
of weather in the mountains

00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:26.390
and poor sanitation in the worker camps.

00:16:26.390 --> 00:16:28.270
Ultimately, over the seven
years of this project,

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:30.990
around 25,000 workers died.

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:32.150
Most of them from cholera

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:34.650
which swept every year
through the workers' camps.

00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:40.730
But vast numbers of laborers,
around 42,000 people a year,

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:42.350
at the height of this project,

00:16:42.350 --> 00:16:44.530
migrated seasonally
across an extensive part

00:16:44.530 --> 00:16:46.250
of Western and Southern India

00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:48.230
to work on the new railway line.

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:51.000
They were mostly landless
peasants and tribal people

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:53.560
who had been relegated to the
margins of Indian society.

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:54.853
Both men and women.

00:16:55.750 --> 00:16:57.060
British railway overseers

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:59.860
recruited and organized
workers ethnographically.

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:01.950
Identifying specific cast communities

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:03.860
with specific labor requirements.

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:06.300
And so, this railway works
was also part of a process

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:10.250
that transformed fluid
regional community identities

00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:11.810
into hardened stereotypes,

00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:14.790
locked into a hierarchy
of skills and status

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:17.763
and a yearly cycle of
migration and contract work.

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:23.620
So, these railway works set
a lot of things in motion

00:17:23.620 --> 00:17:25.350
across the mountains then.

00:17:25.350 --> 00:17:27.673
Capital, commodities and people.

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:30.140
And then on a smaller scale,

00:17:30.140 --> 00:17:33.320
they also sent minerals in motion as well,

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:35.880
because blasting tunnels
through the mountains

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:38.900
in this place, exposed dazzling caves

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:42.480
lined with crystals up to six feet wide.

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:44.317
Even today, these caves
are the world's most,

00:17:44.317 --> 00:17:47.110
they produce the most spectacular examples

00:17:47.110 --> 00:17:48.670
in the world of apophyllite.

00:17:48.670 --> 00:17:50.280
This kind of crystal that only forms

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:52.830
in this kind of volcanic rock.

00:17:52.830 --> 00:17:54.150
Some of the Indian earth workers

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:55.870
who were involved in the railway project

00:17:55.870 --> 00:17:57.420
took large crystals from these caves,

00:17:57.420 --> 00:18:00.380
which the caves became
known as the jewel tunnels.

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:02.780
And they embedded them into
the mud walls of their huts

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:05.883
in the worker's camps as
decoration and as trophy.

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:09.060
The Chief Railway Engineer
who was an Englishman

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:12.250
named James Barkley, had
the largest specimens saved

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:14.890
to distribute to natural history museums.

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:16.050
Some of them stayed within India,

00:18:16.050 --> 00:18:18.140
but most were sent to Britain.

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:20.750
There, they enabled British
scientists and geologists

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:23.910
to develop theories for how
crystals like these form

00:18:23.910 --> 00:18:26.260
following volcanic disturbances.

00:18:26.260 --> 00:18:27.690
And one of these samples

00:18:27.690 --> 00:18:31.080
ended up in the hands of
geologist, Nevil Story Maskelyne,

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:32.240
who was the keeper of minerals

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:33.580
at the British Museum in London.

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:35.510
His name is actually on the
handwritten label there.

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:38.790
You can't really see it, but
it says donor, N.S. Maskelyne.

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:42.033
He eventually gifted it to
Yale where it now resides.

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:46.810
So, this crystal specimen
traveled from deep inside

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:50.040
a mountain in Western India to
the British Museum in London,

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:52.290
and then finally on to New Haven.

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:54.840
And it did so on a journey
shaped by Indian histories

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:57.940
of labor, migration and transportation,

00:18:57.940 --> 00:18:59.545
which transformed an entire landscape

00:18:59.545 --> 00:19:01.650
and shored up British Imperial control

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:03.323
over the Indian sub-continent.

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:06.290
So, this was the story that I had begun

00:19:06.290 --> 00:19:08.640
to delineate for myself
around this object.

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:11.010
The first one that I researched
as part of this project.

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:12.570
And I had assembled this visual archive

00:19:12.570 --> 00:19:14.700
that I mentioned before
of photographs and maps

00:19:14.700 --> 00:19:17.280
and engravings and charts.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:20.000
Just as the pandemic upended
our plans for Garima.

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:21.763
Garima Gupta, the artist.

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:25.020
The original plan was for
her to come to New Haven,

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:26.090
work closely with me,

00:19:26.090 --> 00:19:29.120
observe the objects and
make art as a result.

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:31.520
But amid lockdowns and
travel restrictions,

00:19:31.520 --> 00:19:32.950
we re-conceived the project

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:35.190
as a remote engagement between us

00:19:35.190 --> 00:19:38.170
mediated by high resolution
digital photography,

00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:41.103
cloud databases, Google
documents and Zoom.

00:19:42.500 --> 00:19:44.510
For Garima, whose work typically involves

00:19:44.510 --> 00:19:47.430
extensive field work
and travel across Asia,

00:19:47.430 --> 00:19:49.350
observing and drawing this
specimen of apophyllite

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:52.220
involved a real remaking of her practice.

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:55.190
She felt her way into each
specimen by zooming deep

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:58.220
into the digital images that
the Peabody Museum provided.

00:19:58.220 --> 00:20:01.623
Finding in each one, a kind
of micro landscape of its own.

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:05.110
And when her drawing was complete,

00:20:05.110 --> 00:20:07.773
the main observational
drawing of the specimen,

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:10.140
she then considered its relation

00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:11.970
to the real former landscape

00:20:11.970 --> 00:20:14.270
from which the specimen
had been excavated,

00:20:14.270 --> 00:20:15.960
and to the charts and maps and diagrams

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:17.080
that I'd provided for her

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:19.823
and which we talked about
in extensive conversations.

00:20:21.990 --> 00:20:22.823
And, you know,

00:20:22.823 --> 00:20:24.220
she experimented with placing the drawings

00:20:24.220 --> 00:20:28.820
against retraced maps of the
Bore Ghat railway cuttings.

00:20:28.820 --> 00:20:31.650
And so ultimately, here is the
final work that she created.

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:32.840
The whole work is on the left

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:33.810
and then I've kind of zoomed in

00:20:33.810 --> 00:20:36.360
on the specimen on the right.

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:37.840
And it's displayed on a light box

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:38.940
like I was explaining earlier,

00:20:38.940 --> 00:20:41.820
so that the kind of like
multiple layers of the image

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:43.440
and therefore the layers of history

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:45.083
in this object could emerge.

00:20:46.330 --> 00:20:47.770
And so for each the objects,

00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:50.540
we had a kind of back and forth like this.

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:52.610
In my text that I wrote for each one

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:54.520
and the illustrations that Garima produced

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:57.680
were developed collaboratively
through that process.

00:20:57.680 --> 00:21:00.510
And together, my words
and her illustrations

00:21:00.510 --> 00:21:04.310
engage in a kind of exploratory
practice of placemaking.

00:21:04.310 --> 00:21:05.790
Bringing into view the transformation

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:08.650
of the physical landscape from
which the mineral has come.

00:21:08.650 --> 00:21:10.040
The political and social landscape

00:21:10.040 --> 00:21:11.550
through which it has traveled.

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:14.763
And the surface of the object
as a landscape of its own.

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:17.030
This is just another example,

00:21:17.030 --> 00:21:20.823
a close-up of her drawing of
the Kimberly Mine diamonds.

00:21:22.610 --> 00:21:25.240
The coming into being of
a natural history specimen

00:21:25.240 --> 00:21:27.720
is a transformative
journey in which it sheds

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:31.230
a richer sense of place in
its movement through culture.

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:32.750
And through this collaboration,

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:35.360
we have tried to help these
objects carry their journeys

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:38.826
into the present rather
than leaving them behind.

00:21:38.826 --> 00:21:39.659
Thank you.

00:21:40.507 --> 00:21:43.142
(audience applauding)

00:21:43.142 --> 00:21:45.809
(clears throat)

00:22:00.530 --> 00:22:01.660
- [Audience Member] Can
you talk a little bit more

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:03.683
about sort of,

00:22:06.007 --> 00:22:07.680
other projects like this

00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:09.540
that you've sort of come across

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:11.980
that you'd like to take on other ways

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:13.450
in which you'd like to
see this work at all?

00:22:13.450 --> 00:22:15.060
I mean, it's very exciting
that the exhibitions

00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:17.821
will actually come online, and so.

00:22:17.821 --> 00:22:18.910
- Yeah.

00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:19.743
- [Audience Member] Complete as it were.

00:22:19.743 --> 00:22:20.690
- Yeah.

00:22:20.690 --> 00:22:23.120
Do you mean other projects
here at Yale as part of this?

00:22:23.120 --> 00:22:24.546
- Yeah.
- Or do you mean? Yeah.

00:22:24.546 --> 00:22:26.775
- Related to this.
- Yeah, yeah. Absolutely.

00:22:26.775 --> 00:22:28.530
- [Audience Member] Question
becomes another question.

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:29.780
- Yeah, yeah, definitely.

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:33.050
So while I was at this Yale
Center for British Art,

00:22:33.050 --> 00:22:34.070
I had the opportunity to work

00:22:34.070 --> 00:22:36.140
with a graduate research
assistant on this project,

00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:38.163
kind of investigating
objects and trying to find.

00:22:38.163 --> 00:22:39.240
I had already kind of conceived

00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:40.880
the mineralogy project on my own,

00:22:40.880 --> 00:22:43.500
and I wanted the kind of next stage

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:46.450
to emerge more collaboratively
with other partners at Yale.

00:22:47.970 --> 00:22:51.350
The student that I worked, Manoj Ghadei,

00:22:51.350 --> 00:22:53.640
we eventually came up
with an idea together

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:55.160
that was fully kind of co-conceived,

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:59.100
such that we're now kind of co-curators,

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:01.640
fully equal collaborators
on the next stage of this,

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:02.820
which we're calling,

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:03.970
well, it's a provisional title right now,

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:06.230
but we're calling it,
"The Octant's Journey."

00:23:06.230 --> 00:23:08.020
And so we've now, we've honed in

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:10.380
on an 18th Century
navigational instrument.

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:14.130
That's in the Peabody's
scientific instruments collection.

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:17.953
This is an object that's used
to establish latitude at sea,

00:23:19.090 --> 00:23:22.680
and we are working as
part of an exhibition

00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:23.520
on scientific instruments,

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:25.240
it's gonna happen at the art gallery,

00:23:25.240 --> 00:23:27.709
the Yale University Art
Gallery in a couple of years.

00:23:27.709 --> 00:23:29.490
We're gonna be doing
a kind of intervention

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:33.820
into that exhibition where
this navigational instrument

00:23:33.820 --> 00:23:36.280
basically travels around the exhibition,

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:40.800
encountering objects in it
and narrating its own history

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:42.930
in a printed booklet and on social media.

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:45.360
And what we're doing is
we're playfully adapting

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:47.910
an 18th Century literary genre
called "The It Narrative,"

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:51.740
where objects that are kind of
circulating around the world

00:23:51.740 --> 00:23:54.920
are imbued with narrative power
and tell their own stories.

00:23:54.920 --> 00:23:57.574
And so we're kind of,
we're imagining the octant,

00:23:57.574 --> 00:23:59.140
this navigational instrument

00:23:59.140 --> 00:24:02.240
as a kind of stand in
for the museum visitor.

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:05.670
So, we're dramatizing the
process by which meaning is made

00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:07.620
in a museum through these encounters

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:09.110
between the octant and these objects.

00:24:09.110 --> 00:24:13.640
And the idea is that as it
explores objects at the gallery,

00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:16.430
it is questioning its own role

00:24:16.430 --> 00:24:17.980
as a kind of navigational instrument

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:20.440
from this period of Imperial expansion,

00:24:20.440 --> 00:24:24.033
thinking about its own role
in astronomical expeditions,

00:24:25.180 --> 00:24:26.853
genocide and enslavement,

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:30.543
extractive colonialism.

00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:34.030
And yeah, just trying
to kind of understand

00:24:34.030 --> 00:24:36.073
its own implication in those histories.

00:24:36.930 --> 00:24:37.890
So that's a kind of, you know,

00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:39.710
it's a very different sort
of a project than this.

00:24:39.710 --> 00:24:42.210
But like this, the idea is
to kind of bring objects

00:24:42.210 --> 00:24:44.560
that don't seem to have
anything to do with each other

00:24:44.560 --> 00:24:47.810
and use them to really
question the categories

00:24:47.810 --> 00:24:49.720
that have separated them in museums.

00:24:49.720 --> 00:24:51.370
You know, cause the Yale museums,

00:24:53.180 --> 00:24:54.850
do a lot of collaborative work together,

00:24:54.850 --> 00:24:57.250
but still fundamentally,
they have these different

00:24:57.250 --> 00:24:59.570
kind of conventions of display
and ways of giving meaning

00:24:59.570 --> 00:25:02.760
to objects that illuminate them deeply,

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:04.930
but also hide certain aspects of them.

00:25:04.930 --> 00:25:06.870
And I think one of the great possibilities

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:09.300
of interdisciplinary humanistic work

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:11.320
at a university like Yale
that's had a couple of years

00:25:11.320 --> 00:25:13.440
of collecting history is that they're,

00:25:13.440 --> 00:25:15.150
it's not just that the
collections are rich and deep

00:25:15.150 --> 00:25:16.600
as resources for our own work,

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:19.070
but they are an invitation

00:25:19.070 --> 00:25:22.240
to kind of turn the lens on
the institution themselves

00:25:22.240 --> 00:25:23.927
and ask really important questions about,

00:25:23.927 --> 00:25:25.537
"Why is this object here?

00:25:25.537 --> 00:25:27.537
"Why has it been identified
as this kind of object

00:25:27.537 --> 00:25:29.007
"rather than another kind of object?

00:25:29.007 --> 00:25:31.597
"And what other sorts of
histories might it tell?"

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:33.583
Yeah.

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:39.910
- [Audience Member] Has there been any,

00:25:39.910 --> 00:25:41.310
like with the museums,

00:25:41.310 --> 00:25:43.820
permanently change how
they exhibit them at all?

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:46.217
Like the best looking context is that,

00:25:47.701 --> 00:25:49.533
(mumbles) recommendation of study?

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:53.240
- Yeah. I mean, that's the hope.

00:25:53.240 --> 00:25:54.080
That's the hope, right?

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:57.130
Is to kind of, by collaborating
with the different museums,

00:25:57.130 --> 00:26:00.840
start to inspire changes
and kind of experiments

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:02.550
in how they do things.

00:26:02.550 --> 00:26:04.738
I think maybe another way of describing

00:26:04.738 --> 00:26:08.315
what I'm after in this
project is that I think,

00:26:08.315 --> 00:26:09.900
and now I'm not talking about Yale,

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:12.550
I'm talking about kind of European

00:26:12.550 --> 00:26:14.250
and American museums more broadly.

00:26:15.260 --> 00:26:18.700
The place where this kind
of creative and playful

00:26:18.700 --> 00:26:20.130
and kind of unexpected work happens

00:26:20.130 --> 00:26:21.780
is through artist interventions, right?

00:26:21.780 --> 00:26:23.770
Like people invite the artist to come in,

00:26:23.770 --> 00:26:25.150
engage with the collection

00:26:25.150 --> 00:26:27.930
and then either make work
or select work in a way

00:26:27.930 --> 00:26:31.450
that kind of cuts across the categories

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:32.760
that have structured that collection.

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:34.400
And I really believe in that work

00:26:34.400 --> 00:26:35.610
and I find it really powerful,

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:38.180
but my worry about it
is that it segregates

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:39.300
that kind of critical work

00:26:39.300 --> 00:26:41.610
and says that this is
the work of the artist,

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:45.800
the institution kind of
follows its own kind of dynamic

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:46.970
that is not a part of that.

00:26:46.970 --> 00:26:50.350
And so, you end up displaying
these kinds of interventions

00:26:50.350 --> 00:26:51.760
rather than incorporating them

00:26:51.760 --> 00:26:53.360
into the practice of the museum.

00:26:55.710 --> 00:26:58.050
So, I'm really interested in finding,

00:26:58.050 --> 00:27:00.330
you know, as a curator, I was
trying to do that from within.

00:27:00.330 --> 00:27:02.970
I'm now working from without again,

00:27:02.970 --> 00:27:04.960
but also very much with museums.

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:09.090
And so, my hope is that
we start to really,

00:27:09.090 --> 00:27:12.010
yeah, change the way these
objects' meaning is displayed

00:27:12.010 --> 00:27:13.510
in all these different spaces.

00:27:16.169 --> 00:27:17.232
- Thank you.

00:27:17.232 --> 00:27:18.065
(audience applauding)
- Thanks.

00:27:21.834 --> 00:27:24.251
(soft music)

